27.7 ms. 100% of it in Seq Scan on orders, which reads 200,000 rows to keep 10.

Planning 0.377 ms · Execution 27.7 ms · Buffers 2,417 pages, 56% from cache

Share     Time              Node                Rows     Estimate  Buffers
 100%  27.7 ms  ██████████  Seq Scan on orders    10  1,000 ▼100×    2,417

Findings (2)

  HIGH    ES001 Selective sequential scan
          Seq Scan on orders reads 200,000 rows to keep 10.
          Rows kept: 10 of 200,000 (0.005%)
          Filter: ((orders.customer_id)::text = '4242'::text)
          Table size: 2,417 pages (18.9 MB)
          Time in the node: 27.7 ms (100% of the runtime)
          → The filter applies a cast to customer_id, so an index on customer_id cannot serve it.
          Rewrite the condition so that customer_id stands alone, or index the expression itself.

  LOW     ES002 Row misestimate
          Seq Scan on orders returned 10 rows where the planner expected 1,000: 100× fewer.
          Estimated rows: 1,000
          Actual rows: 10
          → The planner has no statistics for an expression of customer_id, so it guessed. Rewriting
          the condition so that customer_id stands alone fixes the estimate; otherwise CREATE
          STATISTICS on the expression (PostgreSQL 14 and later) gives the planner something to go
          on.

Advice (1)

  SURE    Rewrite the condition on customer_id
          The filter applies a cast to customer_id, so no index on customer_id can serve it. Compare
          customer_id itself with a value of its own type, or a range of values, instead.
          ! An index on the expression itself would also work, but only for an immutable expression
          written exactly as in the query.

Share and Time: time spent in each node itself, wall-clock. Rows: actual rows per loop, ×loops when
it ran more than once. ▲/▼: actual rows above or below the estimate by 10× or more. Buffers: pages
each node read itself.
